================================================================================
Search Performance Implications
================================================================================

  Current (Single-Cell):
    Tests per particle: ~5.9 (direct) + ~29.5 (neighbors)
    Issue: Elements not found when particle crosses to adjacent cell

  Approach A (Block-Wise Leaves):
    Tests per particle: ~35.6 (direct) + ~177.8 (neighbors)
    Pro: Coarser granularity → fewer blocks to search
    Pro: Elements fully contained → better retention
    Con: More tests per block (35.6 vs 5.9)
    Con: Coarser spatial resolution → may search irrelevant blocks

  Approach B (Multi-Cell Vertex Registration):
    Tests per particle: ~23.6 (direct) + ~117.9 (neighbors)
    Pro: Finer granularity → better spatial resolution
    Pro: Elements in all touching cells → better retention
    Con: More memory (135.0 MB vs 16.4 MB)
    Con: More total tests (~141.4 vs ~213.3)

================================================================================
Recommendation
================================================================================

  Approach A (Block-Wise Leaves):
    ✅ Memory: 56.3% REDUCTION
    ✅ Tests per block: 35.6 (reasonable)
    ⚠  Spatial resolution: 16.6% of current
    ✅ Implementation: Simpler (no multi-cell tracking)

  Approach B (Multi-Cell Vertex Registration):
    ❌ Memory: 259.8% INCREASE
    ⚠  Tests per cell: 23.6 (higher)
    ✅ Spatial resolution: 100% (unchanged)
    ⚠  Implementation: More complex (bidirectional mapping)

  Overall Assessment:
    ✅ RECOMMEND Approach B (Multi-Cell Vertex Registration)
       - Better spatial resolution
       - More precise element coverage
       - Worth the memory cost

  Suggested Next Steps:
    1. Implement the recommended approach
